  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Liga Scoreboard",
    "overview": "Every custom order is a game in progress, kept on the hand-flipped scoreboard of a barangay covered court and rebuilt with Apple precision. The instrument surfaces are charcoal boards, like the panel over a gym floor; the readouts on them are true off-white split-flap cards carrying near-black condensed numerals. The shop's logo orange marks the brand and the one next step on a screen, and shot-clock yellow is held back for the things that are happening right now. Everything around the board sits on a warm off-white ground, the paper of the logo, so the board is always the heaviest object in view and the orange the brightest.\n\nThe system is Archivo, worked across its width axis: pulled to its narrowest for board numerals and display, kept at normal width for anything people read. Montserrat SemiBold, set wide, is the brand voice from the logo's tagline, and the small uppercase labels on the boards speak in it. Density follows the scoreboard: a strict cell module, identical cells in a run, ruled bands between readings, lamps for stage. Depth is physical and restrained (a lit, slightly convex board; recessed wells; cards that sit proud of the paint), and the only authored motion is the flip.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Charcoal owns every instrument surface; the ground is warm off-white paper.",
      "Logo orange is the brand and the next step: the badge, one main action per screen, accent type and strokes, focus rings.",
      "Off-white flip cards with a hinge line are the signature readout, never decoration.",
      "Yellow means \"now\": live stage, low stock, money owed, unread. Nothing else.",
      "Archivo differentiated by width (62 / 72 / 85 / normal), weight, and tabular figures; Montserrat only for the brand voice and the board labels.",
      "Light and dark are both first-class, switched by `prefers-color-scheme`.",
      "Every control has press travel; the flip is the single authored animation."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Yellow-Is-Now Rule",
        "body": "Yellow is for live and low: the stage in progress and a size about to run out. On the customer side, money the customer owes right now counts as live: the pay notice's edge and lamp, and the small pay pill on an Orders row, which clear as soon as a payment is waiting for the shop. A shop reply waiting to be read counts too: the chat bubble's unread count, which clears when the chat is opened. So does news about the customer's orders and payments that hasn't been read yet: the notification bell's unread count, which clears as the notifications are read. It never fills a section, a headline, a button at rest, a focus ring, or an illustration chrome. If nothing is live, low, owed, or unread, a screen should show no yellow at all.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One-Orange Rule",
        "body": "Orange is the brand and the next step. Each screen has at most one orange-filled button, its main action; a control that repeats down a list (Make staff on every row) is charcoal, and secondary actions are outlined. Do not introduce another brand hue; supporting surfaces are the warm neutrals, and new states are expressed as card state (lit, yellow, blank) or pick, not new colors.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Card Ink Rule",
        "body": "On yellow use `signal-ink`; on cards use `card-ink`; on charcoal use `board-ink` / `board-ink-2`; on orange use `action-ink`. A field with a problem wears a solid 2px ink ring (`is-error`, `is-invalid`), never a colored one, so it shows on any ground and never reads as the orange focus ring.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Width-Is-Voice Rule",
        "body": "Hierarchy is carried by the width axis and weight of one family, never by a second typeface (Montserrat is the logo's own lettering, kept to the brand voice and the board labels). Board numerals are `wdth` 62, display is 72, labels 85, reading text normal width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Board Rule",
        "body": "Every number that can change (counts, percentages, dates, timestamps, order numbers) is set in tabular figures so the board never reflows.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only-The-Board-Lifts Rule",
        "body": "The large drop shadow belongs to the scoreboard. Other surfaces are flat with a hairline, or recessed as wells.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Flip Rule",
        "body": "The flip is the single authored motion: the top flap falls (170ms, `--ease-in` `cubic-bezier(0.55, 0, 1, 0.45)`, rotateX 0 to -90deg, dimming to 82%), then the bottom flap rises (230ms, `--ease-out` `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`), staggered 28ms per cell. It is interruptible: a new value always starts from the card on screen. The board auto-advances (2.6s) only while at least 35% visible and the tab is visible, and stops for good after a manual tap. Everything else is state transition only (press travel, lamp, cell, nav fade, jersey color cross-fade at 480ms).",
        "section": "components"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** put live data on the board in flip cells with tabular figures and a fixed cell count.",
      "**Do** express inventory state as card state: lit, yellow for low, a blank well slot for sold out on the board, and a hollow ink-ringed slot for sold out off the board.",
      "**Do** keep yellow for live, low, owed, and unread (plus selection and the board-side error hint) only.",
      "**Do** separate the voices with Archivo's width axis: 62 board, 72 display, 85 label, normal for reading.",
      "**Do** give every control press travel and the global orange focus ring.",
      "**Do** ship light and dark together; check `accent`, `pick`, and card values in both.",
      "**Do** honor reduced motion by swapping cards in place, and reduced transparency with a solid nav."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use yellow as a brand fill, a button color at rest, a focus ring, or decoration.",
      "**Don't** put more than one orange-filled button on a screen.",
      "**Don't** add another typeface beyond Archivo and the Montserrat brand voice, or a second brand hue. Don't spread Montserrat into tables, fields or chips: it's too wide for them.",
      "**Don't** animate anything else with an authored entrance, scroll reveal, or loop; the flip is the motion.",
      "**Don't** put the board lift shadow on anything but the scoreboard.",
      "**Don't** set yellow text on light ground; use semibold ink.",
      "**Don't** let a readout reflow when its value changes."
    ]
  }
}
